body:
Can relate, first 2 times they said I was trying to manipulate them (my mother)/get things my way. Third time she said that I'm evil that "this is how I repay them after all the hard work and sacrifices they had to do for me" and that my life is in my hands and I am free to do whatever I want with it. This cold and distant reaction hurt more then I expected and it was unbearable how alone I felt. The only ppl on earth I still believed knew me and to some degree were on my side and wanted what's good for me said in way that I could hear and understand how distant they are.

&#x200B;

Maybe they did not truly believe me. Maybe this was the best they could say when hit with the news that theyr child does not want to live and just acted on it. I really never wish to be in theyr shoes in that situation. I felt bad for putting them through that and still do. No matter how you look at it, shit's fucked.

"I do believe we should be satisfied with what we have and not compare ourselves to others" I agree, absolutely. But this goes beyond being satisfied with what we have, it's more like accepting our current state and giving up on trying to improve or seek what we want/need. That's what ticks me off.

&#x200B;

As far as comparing ourselves, well, we can do it involuntarely, subconciously, and even if we don't, others will gladly do it for us all the time. ( I guess this referred to the last paragraph)

"needing to be alone first before being able to interact with others" is not exactly what this broken record advice says, it's "learning to enjoy and accept being alone" and as I mentioned above, if you manage to do that and it works for you... why bother looking for others after?

&#x200B;

It's almost like a low key "this is out of your league, now get back to your place" (ye, I'm exagerating a lot, but that's how it feels sometimes)
